  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nRest of Today: Surface obs and RAP analysis show a sharp moisture \ngradient, with dew points in the middle 50s north and upper 60s \nsouth, extending from the central UP curling back through far \neastern WI and westward through Marathon county. This boundary may \nservice as the focus for a round of showers/storms this afternoon \nand evening, however, forcing is lacking as convergence along the \nboundary is weak given light surface winds. Should an isolated storm \nor two develop the thermodynamic and kinematic environment would \nsupport a strong and somewhat organized updraft with ~1500 J/KG \nMLCAPE and ~50kts of 0-6km shear. The window for any storms to \ndevelop is 3-8PM.\n\nThe other focus for tonight is the possible return of fog, mainly \nalong and near the lakeshore. Light easterly winds will continue to \nadvect nearly saturated air onshore, so think at least patchy area \nof fog are likely near the lake late tonight into Thursday morning. \nConfidence in how expansive fog becomes is low due to the placement \nof the aforementioned moisture gradient.\n\nPrecip Chances Thursday: Another short-wave is progged to pass \nthrough the region Thursday bring another round of scattered showers \nand storms the region. CAMs generally show showers/storms pushing \ninto central WI after 9-10AM Thursday and continuing on and off \nthrough much of the day. The severe risk for Thursday is very low \n(less than 5%) as ample cloud cover will limit instability and the \nstronger shear is expected to remain over Lake Superior. Main concerns \nwill any storms will be lightning and locally heavy rainfall. HREF \nLPMM does show a few bullseye of 1-1.5\" of rain across central WI. \nThese higher end amounts would be related to any stronger and \nlonger liver storms.\n\nShowers/Storms this Weekend: A progressive upper trough and surface \nlow are forecast to track across the state Saturday into Sunday \nbring another round of scattered to widespread showers/storms. The \ngreatest instability is expected to be shunted well south of the \nforecast area Saturday limiting the severe potential. However, with \nPWATS above the 95th percentile (1.8-2\") there is a first for heavy \nrainfall and isolated flash flooding, mainly south of HWY 29. \nConfidence in the occurrence of heavy rain is low as global ensembles \nvary widely with total precipitation amounts.\n\nNext WeeK Outlook: Dry and seasonal weather is expected to start \nnext week. As the subtropical ridge builds northeast during the \nmiddle and end of next week a more active pattern may develop across \nparts of Wisconsin as a baroclinc zone sets up over the state. Its \nway to early to assess any specific thunderstorm threats, but this \nwill be a time period of monitor, especially for those with outdoor \nplans during the second half of next week.",
